solution:
\textbf{Area of $ABC$.} By Heron's formula with $s=\dfrac{17+25+26}{2}=34$,
\[
[ABC]=\sqrt{34\cdot17\cdot9\cdot8}=\sqrt{41616}=204 .
\]

\textbf{The median triangle has $\dfrac{3}{4}$ of this area.} Let $G$ be the centroid and $D$ the midpoint of $BC$, and extend $GD$ beyond $D$ to $H$ with $DH=GD$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair B=(0,0),C=(26,0),A=(340/52,408/26);
pair D=(B+C)/2,G=(A+B+C)/3,H=2*D-G;
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--D,gray);draw(B--(A+C)/2,gray);draw(C--(A+B)/2,gray);
draw(G--H,blue+linewidth(0.8));draw(B--H,blue+dashed);draw(C--H,dashed+gray);
draw(B--G,blue+linewidth(0.8));
dot(G);dot(D);dot(H);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);
label("$G$",G,dir(70));label("$D$",D,dir(-125));label("$H$",H,S);
label("$17$",A+0.3*(B-A),W);label("$25$",A+0.3*(C-A),NE);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Since the diagonals $BC$ and $GH$ of $BGCH$ bisect each other, $BGCH$ is a parallelogram, so $BH=GC$. The centroid divides each median in the ratio $2:1$, so
\[
BG=\dfrac{2}{3}m_b,\qquad GH=2GD=\dfrac{2}{3}m_a,\qquad BH=GC=\dfrac{2}{3}m_c ,
\]
where $m_a,m_b,m_c$ are the medians from $A,B,C$. So triangle $BGH$ is similar to the median triangle with ratio $\dfrac{2}{3}$. Its area is $[BGH]=2[BGD]=[BGC]=\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]$ (the median $BD$ of triangle $BGH$ halves it, and $D$ is the midpoint of $BC$). Hence the median triangle has area
\[
\left(\dfrac{3}{2}\right)^2\cdot\dfrac{1}{3}[ABC]=\dfrac{3}{4}\cdot204=\boxed{153}.
\]

Let the bisector of $\angle ASP$ meet line $AO$ at $M$. Because $AO\parallel SP$, the alternate angles give $\angle AMS=\angle MSP=\angle ASM$, so triangle $ASM$ is isosceles with $AM=AS$. By the symmetry of the isosceles trapezoid, the bisector from $P$ meets $AO$ at the mirror image of $M$, and the two bisectors meet on $AO$ only if $M$ is on the axis of symmetry, i.e.\ $M$ is the midpoint of $AO$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair Sx=(0,0),P=(36,0),A=(5,12),O=(31,12),M=(18,12);
draw(Sx--P--O--A--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Sx--M,blue+dashed);draw(P--M,blue+dashed);
draw(A--(5,0),dotted);
draw(arc(Sx,3.2,0,degrees(M-Sx)),red);draw(arc(Sx,3.8,degrees(M-Sx),degrees(A-Sx)),red);
draw(arc(P,3.2,degrees(M-P),180),red);draw(arc(P,3.8,degrees(O-P),degrees(M-P)),red);
dot(M);
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$O$",O,NE);label("$P$",P,SE);label("$S$",Sx,SW);label("$M$",M,N);
label("$13$",(A+M)/2,N);label("$13$",(M+O)/2,N);label("$12$",(5,6),E);
label("$36$",(18,0),S);label("$13$",(Sx+A)/2,W);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

So the legs are $AS=OP=AM=13$. The bases differ by $10$, so each leg projects $5$ onto $SP$, and the height is $\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=12$. The area is
\[
\dfrac{26+36}{2}\cdot12=\boxed{372}.
\]

Let the triangle have side $s$ and the circle radius $r$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=4*(pi^(1/3)); real r=sqrt(3)*s^2/(8*pi);
pair P=(0,0),Q=(s,0),R=(s/2,s*sqrt(3)/2);
draw(P--Q--R--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
pair M=(s+1.2+r,r);
draw(circle(M,r),linewidth(0.9));dot(M);draw(M--M+r*dir(30),dashed);
label("$P$",P,SW);label("$Q$",Q,SE);label("$R$",R,N);
label("$s$",(P+Q)/2,S);label("$M$",M,S);label("$r$",M+r/2*dir(30),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The conditions are
\[
\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=2\pi r,\qquad \pi r^2=3s .
\]
From the first, $r=\dfrac{\sqrt3\,s^2}{8\pi}$. Substituting in the second,
\[
\pi\cdot\dfrac{3s^4}{64\pi^2}=3s\ \Longrightarrow\ s^3=64\pi\ \Longrightarrow\ s=4\sqrt[3]{\pi}.
\]
Then
\[
[PQR]=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}s^2=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot16\sqrt[3]{\pi^2}=\boxed{4\sqrt3\,\sqrt[3]{\pi^2}}\approx14.86 .
\]
(Check: $r=\dfrac{\sqrt3\cdot16\sqrt[3]{\pi^2}}{8\pi}=\dfrac{2\sqrt3}{\sqrt[3]{\pi}}$, and $\pi r^2=\dfrac{12\pi}{\sqrt[3]{\pi^2}}=12\sqrt[3]{\pi}=3s$.)

question:
In isosceles right triangle $ABC$ with right angle at $C$, $AC=BC=10$ units. Inside the triangle, horizontal segments (parallel to $BC$) are drawn $1$ unit apart, and vertical segments (parallel to $AC$) are drawn $1$ unit apart, each running from a leg to the hypotenuse $AB$, as shown. Including triangle $ABC$, how many triangles in this figure are isosceles?

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,10),B=(10,0);
for(int i=1;i<10;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,10-i),gray);draw((0,i)--(10-i,i),gray);}
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$10$",(0,5),W);label("$10$",(5,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Put $C$ at the origin with $B=(10,0)$ and $A=(0,10)$. The figure consists of the horizontal lines $y=j$ and the vertical lines $x=i$ ($0\le i,j\le9$, including the legs) together with the hypotenuse $x+y=10$. A triangle needs three sides on three pairwise non-parallel lines, so it uses one horizontal line, one vertical line and the hypotenuse.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair C=(0,0),A=(0,10),B=(10,0);
for(int i=1;i<10;++i){draw((i,0)--(i,10-i),gray);draw((0,i)--(10-i,i),gray);}
fill((3,2)--(8,2)--(3,7)--cycle,paleblue);
draw((3,2)--(8,2)--(3,7)--cycle,blue+linewidth(1));
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot((3,2),blue);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,E);label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$(i,j)$",(3,2),SW,blue);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The lines $x=i$ and $y=j$ meet at $(i,j)$, which lies inside the figure (or on a leg) exactly when $i+j\le9$; the triangle is then the one with vertices $(i,j)$, $(10-j,j)$ and $(i,10-i)$, whose legs both have length $10-i-j$. So every triangle in the figure is an isosceles right triangle, and they correspond to the pairs of integers $i,j\ge0$ with $i+j\le9$. Their number is
\[
10+9+8+\cdots+1=\boxed{55}.
\]

Label the vertices $V_0,\dots,V_5$ in order, with the leash tied at $V_0$; $V_3$ is the opposite vertex. A point $P$ outside the barn is reachable when the shortest path from $V_0$ to $P$ that goes around the barn has length at most $600$. Such a path runs along the boundary, clockwise or counterclockwise, to some vertex $V_k$ and then straight to $P$.

\textbf{Halving the region.} The region is symmetric about the line $V_0V_3$ (dashed). For a point $P$ on the side of $V_1,V_2$, going round that way is never longer, so the half of the region on this side consists of
\begin{itemize}
\item half of the sector at $V_0$: the leash sweeps the exterior angle $240^\circ$ with radius $600$, and half of it is $\dfrac{1}{3}\pi\cdot600^2=120000\pi$;
\item the sector at $V_1$: the leash has $480$ left and sweeps the exterior angle $60^\circ$: $\dfrac{1}{6}\pi\cdot480^2=38400\pi$;
\item the part, on this side of the line $V_0V_3$, of the sector at $V_2$ with radius $360$ and angle $60^\circ$, bounded by the line $V_1V_2$ extended and the side $V_2V_3$ extended (green). Beyond the line $V_0V_3$ the other direction round takes over. A path wrapping round $V_3$ is never needed: going the other way round, via $V_4$, is at least as short there.
\end{itemize}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(8cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real s=1.2, L=6, R=L-2s;
pair[] V; for(int k=0;k<6;++k) V[k]=s*dir(-90+60*k);
path hex=V[0]--V[1]--V[2]--V[3]--V[4]--V[5]--cycle;
real ph=degrees(acos(sqrt(3)/6));
filldraw(arc(V[0],L,150,390)--V[0]--cycle,palegray,gray);
filldraw(V[1]--arc(V[1],L-s,30,90)--cycle,paleblue,gray);
filldraw(V[5]--arc(V[5],L-s,90,150)--cycle,paleblue,gray);
filldraw(V[2]--arc(V[2],R,90,180-ph)--V[3]--cycle,palegreen,gray);
filldraw(V[4]--V[3]--arc(V[4],R,ph,90)--cycle,palegreen,gray);
pair X=V[2]+R*dir(180-ph);
filldraw(hex,white,black+linewidth(1));
draw(V[0]--(0,-L-s),dashed);draw(V[3]--X,dashed);
draw(V[0]--V[0]+L*dir(-60),dashed+gray);draw(V[1]--V[1]+(L-s)*dir(45),dashed+gray);
dot(V[0]);dot(X);
label("$V_0$",V[0],dir(210));label("$V_1$",V[1],dir(-45));label("$V_5$",V[5],dir(225));
label("$V_2$",V[2],E);label("$V_4$",V[4],W);label("$V_3$",V[3],dir(35));label("$X$",X,N);
label("$600$",V[0]+3.5*dir(-60),dir(30));
label("$480$",V[1]+2.6*dir(45),dir(-45));label("$360$",V[2]+(0,2.4),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\textbf{The clipped sector.} Put $V_3$ at the origin with the line $V_3V_0$ as the vertical axis. Then $V_2$ is at horizontal distance $120\cos30^\circ=60\sqrt3$ from the axis, and the sector at $V_2$ consists of the directions from straight up (along $V_1V_2$ extended) to the direction of $V_2V_3$, which makes $30^\circ$ with the horizontal. Measure the angle $\theta$ from the upward vertical towards $V_3$, so $\theta$ runs from $0$ to $60^\circ$. In direction $\theta$ the axis is at distance $\dfrac{60\sqrt3}{\sin\theta}$ from $V_2$, and the arc of radius $360$ meets the axis at $X$, where $\sin\theta_X=\dfrac{60\sqrt3}{360}=\dfrac{\sqrt3}{6}$. The part of the sector on our side is the sector minus the piece beyond the axis:
\[
21600\pi-\int_{\theta_X}^{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\dfrac{1}{2}\left(360^2-\dfrac{10800}{\sin^2\theta}\right)d\theta
=21600\pi-64800\left(\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\theta_X\right)+5400\left(\cot\theta_X-\cot\dfrac{\pi}{3}\right).
\]
Here $\cot\theta_X=\sqrt{11}$, $\cot\dfrac{\pi}{3}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt3}$, and $\dfrac{\pi}{3}-\theta_X=\arctan\sqrt{11}-\dfrac{\pi}{6}$ (because $\dfrac{\pi}{2}-\theta_X$ is the angle whose tangent is $\sqrt{11}$).

\textbf{Total.} The whole area is twice the half:
\[
2\left[120000\pi+38400\pi+21600\pi-64800\left(\arctan\sqrt{11}-\dfrac{\pi}{6}\right)+5400\sqrt{11}-1800\sqrt3\right],
\]
that is,
\[
\boxed{381600\pi-129600\arctan\sqrt{11}+10800\sqrt{11}-3600\sqrt3}\approx1.0628\times10^{6}.
\]
(Adding the full sectors $240000\pi+2\cdot38400\pi+2\cdot21600\pi=360000\pi\approx1.131\times10^6$ would count the overlap of the two sectors behind $V_3$ twice.)

Put $D=(0,0)$, $C=(1,0)$, $B=(1,1)$, $A=(0,1)$. Since $AF=BF$, the point $F$ is on the perpendicular bisector $x=\dfrac{1}{2}$ of $AB$; as $EF\perp DC$, also $E=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},0\right)$. Let $F=\left(\dfrac{1}{2},y\right)$, so $EF=y$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),C=(1,0),B=(1,1),A=(0,1),Ep=(0.5,0),F=(0.5,0.625);
fill(A--D--Ep--F--cycle,paleblue);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(Ep--F,linewidth(0.8));draw(A--F--B,linewidth(0.8));
draw(Ep+(0.05,0)--Ep+(0.05,0.05)--Ep+(0,0.05));
label("$A$",A,NW);label("$B$",B,NE);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$D$",D,SW);
label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$F$",F,dir(-20));
label("$y$",(Ep+F)/2,W);label("$y$",(A+F)/2,SW);
label("$\dfrac{1}{2}$",(0.25,0),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Then $AF=EF$ gives
\[
\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)^2+(1-y)^2=y^2\ \Longrightarrow\ \dfrac{1}{4}+1-2y=0\ \Longrightarrow\ y=\dfrac{5}{8} .
\]
$ADEF$ is a right trapezoid with parallel sides $AD=1$ and $EF=\dfrac{5}{8}$ and width $DE=\dfrac{1}{2}$, so its area is
\[
\dfrac{1}{2}\left(1+\dfrac{5}{8}\right)\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{\dfrac{13}{32}}.
\]

question:
Let $a,b,c$ be nonzero digits (not necessarily different). Let $S$ be the sum of the nine two-digit numbers $\overline{xy}$ obtained by choosing the tens digit $x$ and the units digit $y$ each from $a,b,c$ (all $3\cdot3$ choices, so $\overline{aa},\overline{ab},\overline{ac},\overline{ba},\dots,\overline{cc}$, counted with repetition).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Find $S$ divided by $3$ in terms of $a,b,c$.
\item[(b)] Find all three-digit numbers $\overline{abc}$ for which $S=165$.
\end{enumerate}

Some identical Rubik's cubes are to be given to friends (a friend may get none).
\begin{enumerate}
\item In how many ways can you give: (A) $3$ cubes to $2$ people; (B) $4$ cubes to $2$ people; (C) $5$ cubes to $2$ people; (D) $3$ cubes to $3$ people; (E) $4$ cubes to $3$ people; (F) $5$ cubes to $3$ people?
\item Find, with an explanation, the number of ways to give $7$ cubes to $4$ people.
\item How do the answers to parts 1 and 2 change if each person must get at least one cube?
\end{enumerate}

solution:
Let $AB=x$ for any $x>0$, and put $D=(0,0)$, $E=(1,0)$, $A=(1,\sqrt3)$, $B=(1+x,\sqrt3)$, $C=(2+x,0)$. Then $AD=\sqrt{1+3}=2$, $BC=2$, $AE\perp DC$ with $DE=1$, $AB\parallel DC$ and $AB=x<DC=x+2$. So every $x>0$ gives a trapezoid with all the stated properties; drawing $BE$ adds no condition. Two of them:

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),Ep=(1,0),A=(1,sqrt(3)),B=(3,sqrt(3)),C=(4,0);
draw(D--C--B--A--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ep,dashed);draw(B--Ep,blue);
draw(Ep+(0,0.15)--Ep+(0.15,0.15)--Ep+(0.15,0));
label("$D$",D,SW);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,N);
label("$1$",(D+Ep)/2,S);label("$2$",(D+A)/2,W);label("$2$",(C+B)/2,E);label("$2$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair D=(0,0),Ep=(1,0),A=(1,sqrt(3)),B=(5,sqrt(3)),C=(6,0);
draw(D--C--B--A--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(A--Ep,dashed);draw(B--Ep,blue);
draw(Ep+(0,0.15)--Ep+(0.15,0.15)--Ep+(0.15,0));
label("$D$",D,SW);label("$E$",Ep,S);label("$C$",C,SE);label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,N);
label("$1$",(D+Ep)/2,S);label("$2$",(D+A)/2,W);label("$2$",(C+B)/2,E);label("$4$",(A+B)/2,S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Hence $\boxed{\text{the shorter base can be any positive length}}$; only the relation $DC=AB+2$ is forced.

The student's error is the unjustified assumption that the figure has three equilateral triangles. $\angle EAB=90^\circ$ is true for every $x$ (because $AE\perp DC\parallel AB$), but nothing says that $BE$ bisects an angle or that $\triangle EBC$ is equilateral; that holds only in the special case $x=2$ (left), where $BE=2$. In the right figure, $x=4$, triangle $EBC$ is not equilateral.
